WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.628
Back on the bakery line, the belt now has stations along it.

2
00:00:06.628 --> 00:00:10.163
A slicer takes each loaf, slices it, and puts it back.

3
00:00:10.163 --> 00:00:14.157
One loaf in, one loaf out, right away, and in the same order.

4
00:00:14.157 --> 00:00:16.252
In Reactor, that station is map.

5
00:00:16.967 --> 00:00:18.287
Cakes are different.

6
00:00:18.287 --> 00:00:22.115
Each one needs the icing machine, and icing takes a while.

7
00:00:22.115 --> 00:00:26.010
So our function, icing.ice, doesn't hand back an iced cake.

8
00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:29.772
It hands back a Mono that finishes when the cake is iced.

9
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.863
Here's the question that's easy to be shy about.

10
00:00:32.863 --> 00:00:35.607
map already calls our function for every cake.

11
00:00:35.607 --> 00:00:39.961
Why would we need a different station, just because the function is slow?

12
00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:41.958
Let's just try map.

13
00:00:41.958 --> 00:00:47.054
It calls icing.ice for each cake, and puts whatever comes back on the belt.

14
00:00:47.054 --> 00:00:48.821
What comes back is a Mono.

15
00:00:48.821 --> 00:00:55.072
And remember last time: a Mono is an order form, and nothing happens until someone signs it.

16
00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:59.874
So we get a Flux of Monos: a belt full of unsigned order forms.

17
00:00:59.874 --> 00:01:00.600
Very tidy.

18
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:01.907
Not one cake iced.

19
00:01:01.907 --> 00:01:09.023
The compiler is happy, a test that counts items is happy, and the icing machine never switches on.

20
00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:14.433
So the point is: if your function returns a Mono, map is the wrong station.

21
00:01:14.433 --> 00:01:16.096
The type even tells you.

22
00:01:16.096 --> 00:01:18.939
A Flux of Monos means you wanted flatMap.

23
00:01:19.767 --> 00:01:23.023
So picture an icing room next to the belt, with a hatch.

24
00:01:23.023 --> 00:01:26.221
Each cake goes through the hatch as soon as it arrives.

25
00:01:26.221 --> 00:01:28.082
Several decorators work at once.

26
00:01:28.082 --> 00:01:31.804
And iced cakes come back out in whatever order they're finished.

27
00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:32.880
Let's map it.

28
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.248
The hatch is flatMap.

29
00:01:34.248 --> 00:01:39.393
Each cake's trip, the Mono our function returns, is called the inner publisher.

30
00:01:39.393 --> 00:01:47.079
flatMap subscribes to each inner right away, without waiting for the others, and merges what comes back onto one belt.

31
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:48.461
Let's shrink it.

32
00:01:48.461 --> 00:01:53.467
Three cakes, numbered 3, 1 and 2, reach the hatch at the same moment.

33
00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:57.240
Cake number i takes i times 100 milliseconds to ice.

34
00:01:57.467 --> 00:02:01.612
What comes out of flatMap, in what order, and when is the last cake done?

35
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:09.263
Cake 1 is done at 100 milliseconds, and goes out at once.

36
00:02:09.263 --> 00:02:10.899
Then cake 2, at 200.

37
00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:12.535
Then cake 3, at 300.

38
00:02:12.535 --> 00:02:17.034
So the output is 1, 2, 3, all done at 300 milliseconds.

39
00:02:17.034 --> 00:02:21.124
The order follows finishing time, not input order.

40
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:23.663
Now let's break the picture a little.

41
00:02:23.663 --> 00:02:25.237
Who are these decorators?

42
00:02:25.237 --> 00:02:29.204
It's tempting to think flatMap hires them, one thread per cake.

43
00:02:29.433 --> 00:02:30.188
It doesn't.

44
00:02:30.188 --> 00:02:34.788
flatMap creates no threads, and the lesson's demo shows both cases.

45
00:02:34.788 --> 00:02:39.800
With inners that finish right away, every cake is iced on main, in order.

46
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:47.145
With Mono.delay inners, timers that finish on Reactor's own threads, cake 3 comes out first, on parallel-3.

47
00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:52.684
So the decorators are the inners themselves: a timer, or a call to another computer.

48
00:02:52.684 --> 00:02:55.406
flatMap only subscribes to several at once.

49
00:02:55.406 --> 00:02:59.077
It does none of the icing, and still takes all the credit.

50
00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:01.501
And how many at once?

51
00:03:01.501 --> 00:03:02.797
By default, 256.

52
00:03:02.797 --> 00:03:04.418
flatMap is generous.

53
00:03:04.418 --> 00:03:09.440
It would send 256 cakes to an icing machine with four nozzles.

54
00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.167
Generous isn't the word the machine would use.

55
00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.121
So pass a second number, the concurrency.

56
00:03:16.121 --> 00:03:19.571
flatMap with 4 keeps at most 4 cakes at the machine.

57
00:03:19.571 --> 00:03:26.870
And it does that with slips: its first request to the source is for 4, then one more each time a cake is done.

58
00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:32.179
In the demo, with a limit of 2, the source saw a slip for 2, then 1, 1, 1 and 1.

59
00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.865
So set the concurrency to what the other side can take, not to a feeling.

60
00:03:37.667 --> 00:03:40.226
But sometimes order is the whole point.

61
00:03:40.226 --> 00:03:41.669
How do we get it back?

62
00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:47.683
concatMap is one decorator, who finishes each cake before taking the next.

63
00:03:47.683 --> 00:03:49.480
Same cakes: 3, 1 and 2.

64
00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:52.763
The output keeps the input order, 3, 1, 2.

65
00:03:52.763 --> 00:03:59.015
But the times add up, 300, then 100, then 200, so it's done at 600 milliseconds.

66
00:03:59.233 --> 00:04:03.706
flatMapSequential is the icing room with a clerk at the exit.

67
00:04:03.706 --> 00:04:06.493
All three go in at once, like flatMap.

68
00:04:06.493 --> 00:04:10.526
But the clerk holds cakes 1 and 2 until cake 3 is done.

69
00:04:10.526 --> 00:04:13.532
Output 3, 1, 2, done at 300 milliseconds.

70
00:04:13.733 --> 00:04:15.185
That speed has a price.

71
00:04:15.185 --> 00:04:17.774
Cakes that finish early wait in a buffer.

72
00:04:17.774 --> 00:04:24.970
And one slow cake at the front holds back every cake behind it, like one customer at the till, counting out coins.

73
00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:27.243
That's called head-of-line blocking.

74
00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:28.276
One warning.

75
00:04:28.276 --> 00:04:31.714
flatMapSequential orders the results, not the work.

76
00:04:31.714 --> 00:04:38.388
The layers of a tall cake need concatMap, because each layer must wait until the one below has set.

77
00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.226
Here it is in Java and Kotlin.

78
00:04:41.226 --> 00:04:46.899
In Kotlin, the lambda stays inside the parentheses when another argument follows it.

79
00:04:46.899 --> 00:04:53.044
And Kotlin's own Flow starts from the other side: one at a time by default, like concatMap.

80
00:04:53.867 --> 00:04:56.529
So, why a different station for slow work?

81
00:04:56.529 --> 00:05:00.459
Because map passes the order form along, and flatMap signs it.

82
00:05:00.459 --> 00:05:04.833
flatMap is the icing room: fast, in any order, with a cap you choose.

83
00:05:04.833 --> 00:05:08.827
When order matters, pick one decorator, or a clerk at the exit.

84
00:05:09.033 --> 00:05:10.070
Quick check.

85
00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:12.922
Cakes A, B and C arrive together.

86
00:05:12.922 --> 00:05:17.675
A takes 300 milliseconds, B takes 100, and C takes 200.

87
00:05:17.675 --> 00:05:19.663
What does flatMap emit?

88
00:05:22.633 --> 00:05:23.877
B, then C, then A.

89
00:05:23.877 --> 00:05:29.473
Each one leaves as soon as it's iced, and the stream is done at 300 milliseconds.
